1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,430
You've read an English sentence up to one hidden character.

2
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:07,440
With yes-or-no questions, how many do you need, on average?

3
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:16,313
The natural answer is five: each answer halves what's left, and five cuts take twenty-seven to one.

4
00:00:16,966 --> 00:00:21,856
The ideal is log base two of twenty-seven: four point seven five.

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:26,980
In 1951, Claude Shannon put the true figure at about one.

6
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:33,770
No compressor beats it on average, and guessing what comes next is how language models are trained.

7
00:00:34,266 --> 00:00:37,056
So where do four of every five questions go?

8
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:42,976
Z costs as much as E, yet E turns up a hundred thirty times as often.

9
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:49,480
Instead, split the probability, not the alphabet: is it a space, E, T, A or O?

10
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:51,560
That's nearly half.

11
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:53,823
E starts near a tenth.

12
00:00:54,123 --> 00:00:58,723
Each ideal answer keeps half the probability, so E's share doubles.

13
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:01,303
Three point three doublings fill the bar.

14
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:05,670
Z, one in twelve hundred sixty, needs ten point three.

15
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:12,643
A character with probability p needs k questions, where p times two to the k equals one.

16
00:01:14,143 --> 00:01:20,573
So two to the k equals one over p, and k equals log base two of one over p.

17
00:01:21,066 --> 00:01:23,526
Now make each character's cost its height.

18
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:27,753
The widths add to one, so the area is the average cost.

19
00:01:28,053 --> 00:01:31,453
Pour the skyline flat, and its height is that average.

20
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:36,336
Shannon called it entropy, in bits: one bit per yes-or-no answer.

21
00:01:36,636 --> 00:01:39,256
For English letters: four point one one.

22
00:01:40,566 --> 00:01:47,576
That beats four point seven five, but it's four times Shannon's estimate: it ignores what came before.

23
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:49,610
What comes after a Q?

24
00:01:51,733 --> 00:01:54,373
It's U, ninety-eight percent of the time.

25
00:01:54,673 --> 00:01:59,143
U swallows the bar, and the average falls to point one six bits.

26
00:02:00,366 --> 00:02:05,286
After TH, E takes two thirds of the bar, and costs about half a question.

27
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:16,950
Half a question sounds impossible, but a compressor asks about long runs of letters, and the fractions add up: five questions for these three, not six.

28
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:20,460
After TH, it averages one point seven.

29
00:02:21,133 --> 00:02:25,783
But averaged over every two-letter context, it's still two point eight.

30
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:34,813
Hundred-letter contexts need more text than any corpus holds, so Shannon used a predictor that knows English: a person.

31
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:44,223
His subject read a passage letter by letter, guessing each next one until right: seventy-nine of a hundred two on the first guess.

32
00:02:45,633 --> 00:02:49,893
A twin guessing the same way could rebuild the passage from these counts.

33
00:02:50,193 --> 00:02:51,983
Treat each count as a symbol:

34
00:02:52,983 --> 00:02:56,723
the same skyline pours flat at about one point four bits.

35
00:02:57,223 --> 00:02:59,323
A better guesser could only lower it.

36
00:02:59,833 --> 00:03:06,853
With a hundred letters of context, Shannon's tests put English between point six and one point three bits.

37
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:19,030
In twenty twenty-three, a language model compressing part of a book that reached Project Gutenberg after its training needed point eight four bits, inside that range.

38
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:23,303
The hidden character from the start is the Y in the other day.

39
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:26,793
Shannon's subject needed one guess: is it Y?

40
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:29,333
Splitting the alphabet took five.

41
00:03:29,833 --> 00:03:33,373
In English, almost no letter arrives without a past.

